sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch05-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A cylindrical cell such as Spirogyra that grows without external restraint takes its form because its developing membrane is anisotropic, extending more easily in one direction; a slight inequality of tension in two directions gives an ellipsoidal cell and a great inequality a cylindrical one.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Freely growing cylindrical cells owe their form to an anisotropic wall";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter V, “The Forms of Cells”; topic “Anisotropic walls and elongated cells”. Scope: elongated plant cells, algae, yeasts and other walled cells grown free of external restraint"
}
